  "review_text": "Six graded stories, spread out over 5 issues (once again <i>Strange Tales</i> had two), resulting in a weighted average of [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=2.81], by <i>far</i> the worst month Marvel has ever had in terms of quality, and a collapse of nearly 1.5 points from April's modest and already unimpressive <b>4.29</b> score, shockingly with <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> this time being the worst of the bunch.\n\nEven with six stories, the entire month's output barely exceeded three full issues' worth of content (3.25).\n\nFor the first time in its history, not a single book rose even as high as reader level (4.0 -- the absolute baseline for an enjoyable, but still-not exceptional read), though a couple came close (<i>Fantastic Four</i> and the Doctor Strange half of <i>Strange Tales</i>).&nbsp; With no <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> issue to help anchor the month, the line's structural weaknesses were exposed.\n\nThere's no getting around the fact that this was an absolutely <i>dreadful</i> showing and not a good look for the new \"Marvel Age of Comics\" that the company so prominently displayed on each of its covers this month.\n\nIt's a month where Marvel loudly declared its <i>ambition</i>, but then delivered <i>none of the substance</i> to back it up, the clearest early example of the company's reach exceeding its grasp\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** SUMMARY_BEGIN ***\n*** THIS MONTH ***6 graded stories over 5 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 3.25 grade character issue   weight 3.5   ff        FF #17  1.00 3.5   strange   ST #111 0.25 3.0   torch     ST #111 0.50 2.5   ant       TTA #46 0.50 2.5   iron      TOS #44 0.50 1.5   thor      JIM #94 0.502.81*** LAST MONTH ***7 graded stories over 6 issues...TOTAL WEIGHT: 4.25 grade character issue   weight 9.0   asm       ASM #3  1.00 5.0   strange   ST #110 0.25 3.5   thor      JIM #93 0.50 3.0   ant       TTA #45 0.50 3.0   ff        FF #16  1.00 2.0   torch     ST #110 0.50 1.5   iron      TOS #43 0.504.29\n*** SUMMARY_END ***]",
  "letters_page": "[HANG0] * The introduction of longtime recurring character of Baron Mordo was the lone highlight of the month...and take it from me -- it's a stretch to call that a highlight!&nbsp; All other introductions were of one-and-done types.\n\n[DEVNOTE: *** INTRODUCTION_BEGIN ***\nNotes | first | villain | Cyclops\nNotes | first | villain | A-Chiltarians\nNotes | first | villain | Kraglin\nNotes | first | villain | Asbestos Man (Orson Kasloff)\nNotes | first | villain | Blackie Barker\nBaron Mordo | villain | Milestone: (first)\nNotes | first | Cleopatra\nNotes | first | villain | death | Mad Pharaoh (Hatap)\n*** INTRODUCTION_END ***]\n\n<b>Roundup:</b>\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_BEGIN ***]\n[HANG0] * Thor gets bonked on the head, destroys the world with Loki, and everything's magically fine again by the end of page 13.\n[HANG0] * Bored heroes, a Cyclops robot, and a Greek guy named George still can't wake up this Ant-Man/Wasp story.\n[HANG0] * The Fantastic Four show up ready for adventure, but Doom's floating lab has more lift than the story they're stuck in.\n[HANG0] * The Human Torch is outmaneuvered by headlines and an old castle, more than his asbestos-themed opponent.\n[HANG0] * Doctor Strange gets a new foe in Baron Mordo -- and a whole lot of unanswered questions to go along with him.\n[HANG0] * Iron Man takes a wild detour into ancient Egypt, but the plot (and lack of a dictionary) are the real villains.\n[DEVNOTE: *** HIGHLIGHTS_END ***]",
  "ownership": "[HANG0] * Thor faces off against...<i>himself</i>?\n[HANG0] * Spider-Man is back to face a sandy new menace, not to mention a full-blown team-up with the Human Torch\n[HANG0] * the Skrulls are back to pester the FF\n[HANG0] * and Stan rolls out the carpet for some supporting characters for Iron Man!"
}